A||||10||pHoOl||Squealing on startup||||4||
Z||000000||pHoOl||07-17-2000||10:19 PM||||My 88GT is making some odd noises shortly after starting.  It sounds kinda like a belt squeal, but not quite.  It also changes in pitch slightly.  I'm not hip to all of the automotive lingo, but i think that it's the belt to the right and towards the trunk of the engine.  I believe this has the alternator in the loop.  I thought it might be a loose belt, so i added some belt dressing, but it didn't seem to work.  The noise seems to stop after the car has been running for a while.  I've seen people do picture attachments, so I'll see if i can find a way to do a .wav attachment.  Until i get that going, any ideas?||||reg||4
Z||000001||fiero56||07-17-2000||10:24 PM||||Watch the volt-meter in the rally guage pod(above the radio). If the alternator belt is slipping, the needle will hang low until the belt catches.||||reg||1
Z||000002||87GTZ34||07-17-2000||10:31 PM||||My '87 also did this.  I discovered that coolant was leaking on the belt causing it to squeal.  It would stop as soon as heat buildup would dry it out.<P>------------------<BR>87GTZ34||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||07-17-2000||10:41 PM||||My 86 was also doing this. Belt dressing didnt help either, but it was the alternator belt, All it needed was thightning<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT<BR>2.8 w/Twin Turbos||||reg||1
Z||000004||pHoOl||07-19-2000||11:58 PM||||I'll have to try tightening that belt.  Seanh, twin turbos?  one isn't enough?  I'd like to add turbo at some point, is it expensive/easy to do?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||07-20-2000||02:05 AM||||I would pull off the belts and check the pulleys. You could have a bad bearing in alt or water pump. Also you could then check the belts closely for wear and clean the pulley grooves. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Seanh||07-20-2000||06:40 PM||||Well, I didnt install the turbos, so Im not sure how difficult it is. It has an aftermarket deck lid that has a small buldge just for the purpose of the twins in there. It dont look that bad, actualy hardly noticable. I think that the twin system and engine is going to be taken out and a new Northstar V8 and one of the turbos is going on it, if not both. That will be the ultiment Fiero GT. But, If you buy the turbos new, they ARE expensive. And they look fiarly difficult to install. So, Its up to you. I would have a local grage put them in if you dont have much experiance, or lucky enough to find a kit for a Fiero engine. <P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8||||reg||1
Z||000007||2birds||07-21-2000||01:22 AM||||I've had the squeal off and on for years.  My causes have been a leaking water pump (replace it), spilled anti-freeze on the belt (replace belt and be more careful next time you change the thermostat), loose belt (tighten it) and the front crank seal wearing a groove in the balancer (balancer sleeve kit by Fel-pro, around $8 and new front seal, around $10-20).<BR>I prefer Goodyear Gatorback belts, under $10 from Summitt, more than $20 from my local Goodyear dealer.<P>I wish there was a way to put an automatic tensioner on a 2.8. <p>[This message has been edited by 2birds (edited 07-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Bugsy||07-25-2000||11:24 PM||||my 86 SE v-6 did the squeal routine when cold, and when the alt was under load, I couldn't run the air or roll down the windows at idle speed. I replaced the alt and the belt, and have no more problems<P>------------------<BR>86 SE I'm addicted and proud of it<BR>Kevin||||reg||1
Z||000009||pHoOl||07-26-2000||12:44 AM||||come to think of it, we did replace the thermostat, and i dont' remember it sqealing before we replaced the thermostat.  Maybe i just need a new belt ||||reg||1
